NNAAMMEE
     hhoosstt — DNS lookup utility

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     hhoosstt [--4466aaCCddiillrrssTTVVvvww] [--cc _c_l_a_s_s] [--mm _f_l_a_g] [--NN _n_d_o_t_s] [--RR _n_u_m_b_e_r]
          [--tt _t_y_p_e] [--WW _w_a_i_t] _n_a_m_e [_s_e_r_v_e_r]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The hhoosstt command is a simple utility for performing DNS lookups.  It is
     normally used to convert names to IP addresses and vice versa.

     _n_a_m_e is the domain name that is to be looked up.  It can also be a dot‐
     ted-decimal IPv4 address or a colon-delimited IPv6 address, in which case
     hhoosstt will by default perform a reverse lookup for that address.  _s_e_r_v_e_r
     is an optional argument which is either the name or IP address of the
     name server that hhoosstt should query instead of the server or servers
     listed in _/_e_t_c_/_r_e_s_o_l_v_._c_o_n_f.

     The options are as follows:

     --44      Use IPv4 only for query transport.

     --66      Use IPv6 only for query transport.

     --aa      "All".  The --aa option is normally equivalent to --vv --tt AANNYY.  It
             also affects the behaviour of the --ll list zone option.

     --CC      Check consistency: hhoosstt will query the SOA records for zone _n_a_m_e
             from all the listed authoritative name servers for that zone.
             The list of name servers is defined by the NS records that are
             found for the zone.

     --cc _c_l_a_s_s
             Query class: This can be used to lookup HHSS (Hesiod) or CCHH (Chaos‐
             net) class resource records.  The default class is IINN (Internet).

     --dd      Print debugging traces.  Equivalent to the --vv verbose option.

     --ii      Obsolete.  Use the IP6.INT domain for reverse lookups of IPv6 ad‐
             dresses as defined in RFC 1886 and deprecated in RFC 4159.  The
             default is to use IP6.ARPA as specified in RFC 3596.

     --ll      List zone: The hhoosstt command performs a zone transfer of zone _n_a_m_e
             and prints out the NS, PTR and address records (A/AAAA).

             Together, the --ll --aa options print all records in the zone.

     --mm _f_l_a_g
             Memory usage debugging: the flag can be rreeccoorrdd, uussaaggee, or ttrraaccee.
             You can specify the --mm option more than once to set multiple
             flags.

     --NN _n_d_o_t_s
             The number of dots that have to be in _n_a_m_e for it to be consid‐
             ered absolute.  The default value is that defined using the ndots
             statement in _/_e_t_c_/_r_e_s_o_l_v_._c_o_n_f, or 1 if no ndots statement is
             present.  Names with fewer dots are interpreted as relative names
             and will be searched for in the domains listed in the _s_e_a_r_c_h or
             _d_o_m_a_i_n directive in _/_e_t_c_/_r_e_s_o_l_v_._c_o_n_f.

     --RR _n_u_m_b_e_r
             Number of retries for UDP queries: If _n_u_m_b_e_r is negative or zero,
             the number of retries will default to 1.  The default value is 1.

     --rr      Non-recursive query: Setting this option clears the RD (recursion
             desired) bit in the query.  This should mean that the name server
             receiving the query will not attempt to resolve _n_a_m_e.  The --rr op‐
             tion enables hhoosstt to mimic the behavior of a name server by mak‐
             ing non-recursive queries and expecting to receive answers to
             those queries that can be referrals to other name servers.

     --ss      Do _n_o_t send the query to the next name server if any server re‐
             sponds with a SERVFAIL response, which is the reverse of normal
             stub resolver behavior.

     --TT      TCP: By default, hhoosstt uses UDP when making queries.  The --TT op‐
             tion makes it use a TCP connection when querying the name server.
             TCP will be automatically selected for queries that require it,
             such as zone transfer (AXFR) requests.

     --tt _t_y_p_e
             Query type: The _t_y_p_e argument can be any recognized query type:
             CCNNAAMMEE, NNSS, SSOOAA, TTXXTT, DDNNSSKKEEYY, AAXXFFRR, etc.

             When no query type is specified, hhoosstt automatically selects an
             appropriate query type.  By default, it looks for A, AAAA, and MX
             records.  If the --CC option is given, queries will be made for SOA
             records.  If _n_a_m_e is a dotted-decimal IPv4 address or colon-de‐
             limited IPv6 address, hhoosstt will query for PTR records.

             If a query type of IIXXFFRR is chosen, the starting serial number can
             be specified by appending an equal followed by the starting se‐
             rial number (like --tt IIXXFFRR==_1_2_3_4_5_6_7_8).

     --VV      Print the version number and exit.

     --vv      Verbose output.  Equivalent to the --dd debug option.

     --WW _w_a_i_t
             Timeout: Wait for up to _w_a_i_t seconds for a reply.  If _w_a_i_t is
             less than one, the wait interval is set to one second.

             By default, hhoosstt will wait for 5 seconds for UDP responses and 10
             seconds for TCP connections.

     --ww      Wait forever: The query timeout is set to the maximum possible.

